<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vyazma</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vyazma is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> Oblast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, on the Vyazma river on the road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> to Smolensk. Seat of a small principality from the 13th century, it was taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> in 1403 and by Moscow in 1494. Russian forces fought the retreating French there in 1812, and in October 1941 Soviet armies were encircled nearby.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vyazma.jpg" alt="Vyazma" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stoljaroff · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pyh%C3%A4n_Kolminaisuuden_kirkko_Vjazmassa_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Vyazma lies on the Vyazma River about halfway between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozhaysk">Mozhaysk</a>, and it is the administrative centre of Vyazemsky District in Smolensk Oblast. The river's name comes from the Russian word vyaz', meaning bog or swamp. Throughout its history the town helped defend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> against invasions from the west aimed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, and it is today a major railway junction on the line from Moscow to Smolensk.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The territory around Vyazma was rapidly developed in the 12th century, and a fortified settlement was built in the late 12th or early 13th century. The chronicles first mention it under the year 1230, when it was given as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> to Andrey Vladimirovich. It became an important administrative centre of the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> and a centre of trade and crafts, and from the late 13th century of a separate appanage principality. In 1403 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> expelled the local princes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, where they became service princes and took the name Vyazemsky. A Russian army under Daniil Shchenya captured the town in 1494, and the Treaty of Deulino of 1618 confirmed it as Russian after the devastation of the Time of Troubles.</p><p>A kremlin wall with six towers was built from 1631 to 1634, and the Spasskaya Tower survives. During the Russo-Polish War of 1654–1667 the town was the main stronghold in the struggle for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a>, and Tsar Alexis lived there in the winter of 1654–1655 to escape the plague.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>On 22 October 1812 Russian forces under Mikhail Miloradovich and Matvey Platov attacked the rear guard of the retreating French army, under Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-nicolas-davout">Louis-Nicolas Davout</a>, near Vyazma. The French lost 6,000 men and 2,500 were taken prisoner, while the Russians lost around 2,000. In October 1941 four Soviet armies were surrounded west of the town by German forces, who occupied it from 7 October 1941 to 12 March 1943. The Germans ran camps there, Dulag 184 and Dulag 230, where tens of thousands of prisoners are estimated to have died, and 117 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were shot in December 1941. The town was heavily damaged and rebuilt, and a memorial complex stands outside it on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a> highway.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vyazma” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Vyazma</b>, on the upper Vyazma river east of Smolensk, is first mentioned in the 13th century as a town of the Principality of Smolensk.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-vyazma">Principality of Vyazma</a></li></ul></section>
